\alttext(Two matched pairs, W0 to T1 at cost two and W1 to T0 at cost one, with their computed row and column potentials.)

> Why prices certify an assignment

A permutation \(\pi\) chooses exactly one task for each row. Its cost is
\(P=\sum_i c_{i,\pi(i)}\). Introduce prices \(u_i,v_j\) obeying
\(u_i+v_j\le c_{ij}\). For every permutation,

$$
P\ge\sum_i(u_i+v_{\pi(i)})=\sum_i u_i+\sum_j v_j=D.
$$
\alttext(The cost of any permutation is at least the sum of feasible row and column prices.)

If every selected edge is tight, \(c_{i,\pi(i)}=u_i+v_{\pi(i)}\), the inequality
is equality: the assignment attains a lower bound for every other permutation.
This elementary weak-duality argument is enough for an exact optimality
certificate; no general linear-programming solver is needed.
`assignment_diagnostics` computes the primal, dual, their signed gap, the
minimum slack over all cells and the largest absolute matched-edge slack. These
are floating diagnostics, not an exact certificate for arbitrary real input.

The compact kernel starts with zero prices and adds rows one at a time. In a
search let S be its reached rows and T its expanded real columns. It stores the
smallest slack to each column outside T and chooses their minimum delta. Add
delta to prices of S and subtract it from prices of T. Slack on S×T is
unchanged; slack from S to outside T decreases by delta and stays nonnegative;
slack from outside S into T increases by delta. An exposed tight column either
leads to its matched row or ends an augmenting path. Flipping that path adds one
matched row without losing tightness.

For nonnegative costs the zero initialization is globally feasible. With
negative costs, zero prices can violate an unintroduced row. The correct
invariant is feasibility for the completed prefix. The newly introduced row's
first delta may be negative; its first repricing makes that row feasible too.
Later deltas are nonnegative in exact arithmetic. The prefix then grows by one.
The table below deliberately includes a negative first delta; it is valid.

> Preserve the original three-worker example

The original costs are \(((9,2,7),(6,4,3),(5,8,1))\). The answer remains
\(\pi=(1,0,2)\), costing nine. The runner-up permutation costs ten. Individually
cheapest choices collide at task two for workers one and two, which explains why
separate row minima do not solve the constrained problem. The figure prints
every original cost and highlights the selected cells.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hungarian_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto c = dm::algorithms::assignment_example_costs();
    const auto r = dm::algorithms::hungarian_run(c);
    std::print("| worker | selected task | selected cost | row price |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < c.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, r.result.assignment[i],
                   c[i][r.result.assignment[i]], r.row_potentials[i]);
    const auto d = dm::algorithms::assignment_diagnostics(c, r);
    std::print("Primal {}, dual {}, gap {}, minimum slack {}, matched residual {}.\n",
               d.primal, d.dual, d.gap, d.minimum_slack,
               d.maximum_matched_residual);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> x, y, sx, sy;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < c.size(); ++i) {
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < c.size(); ++j) {
            x.push_back(j);
            y.push_back(2. - i);
        }
        sx.push_back(r.result.assignment[i]);
        sy.push_back(2. - i);
    }
    p.points(x, y, "all costs", {203, 213, 225}, 25).points(sx, sy, "selected",
                                                            {37, 99, 235}, 31);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < c.size(); ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < c.size(); ++j)
            p.text(j, 2. - i, std::format("{:g}", c[i][j]), {15, 23, 42}, 16);
    p.title("Original assignment: total cost 9").x_label("task index").y_label(
        "2 minus worker index (W0 at top)").x_limits(-.5, 2.5).y_limits(-.5, 2.8).save_svg("build/hungarian_original.svg");
}
```
