> Negative costs, limits and ownership

The next example starts with a negative minimum. Finite negative values are
supported without shifting or rescaling the matrix; maximizing a finite value
matrix can be done by negating its entries and reversing the total sign. Ties
use ascending column order and strict-less updates, preserving the old choice
rule, but the returned optimum need not be unique.

Input is nonempty, square and entirely finite. NaN and infinity are rejected
before the search. They are not forbidden-edge markers. Nonfinite produced
slack, potentials, frontier updates or row-order total throw rather than return
a damaged result or leave the search without a valid next column. Large finite
inputs can therefore fail even when their abstract assignment has an optimum;
this solver neither rescales automatically nor supplies exact rational
arithmetic.

`AssignmentOptions` defaults to one million complete input cells and one million
logical history entries. Zero disables the respective optional cap. Every event
costs 1+4n entries: one event plus the row prices, column prices, column owners
and reached-column flags. The complete event is admitted before its producing
scan/reprice or path flip. Units exclude the owned input copy,
ordinary/transient arrays, final outputs, vector capacity, scalar metadata and
byte/peak memory. An exception returns no partial history. The legacy
`hungarian` entry point disables both optional caps; `hungarian_run` ignores the
history cap. Trace data own their values and do not borrow the source matrix.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hungarian_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::hungarian_trace({{-3, 2}, {-1, 0}});
    const auto d = dm::algorithms::assignment_diagnostics(t.costs, t.run);
    std::print("| control | observed |\n| --- | --- |\n| negative first delta | {} |\n| final primal | {} " "|\n| final dual | {} |\n| final gap | {} |\n",
               t.events.front().delta, d.primal, d.dual, d.gap);
    const std::size_t units = t.events.size() * 9;
    (void) dm::algorithms::hungarian_trace(t.costs, {4, units});
    std::print("| exact complete event units | {} |\n", units);
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hungarian_trace(t.costs, {4, units - 1});
        throw std::runtime_error("cap accepted");
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        std::print("| one-short history cap | rejected |\n");
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hungarian({{std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()}});
        throw std::runtime_error("infinity accepted");
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        std::print("| infinite input | rejected |\n");
    }
    const auto tie = dm::algorithms::hungarian({{0, 0}, {0, 0}});
    std::print("| all-zero tie assignment | {} {} |\n", tie.assignment[0],
               tie.assignment[1]);
    dm::algorithms::assignment_diagram(t.costs, t.run).save_svg(
        "build/hungarian_controls.svg");
}
```
