\caption(Different cost structures produce quadratic and cubic slack-evaluation counts under the same solver.)
\alttext(Actual slack evaluations for sizes two through sixty-four; the all-tied family reaches 89440 while the diagonal family reaches 4096.)

> Validation and sources

Focused sanitizer checks compare all 19683 three-by-three matrices with entries
minus one, zero and one against exhaustive permutation costs, and check every
captured active-prefix feasibility and matched-edge tightness condition. The
original solver tests, explicit path replacement, stable ties, complete caps,
ownership, malformed input, nonfinite values and arithmetic overflow are checked
separately. Frozen comparisons preserve 20000 signed dyadic assignments and
every bit of their row-order totals. An independent integer-arithmetic Python
kernel checks 761 cases and 13519 actual events, including all selected work
counts. Fresh coverage checks pass all 55 definitions, compiled public examples
and representative clangd hovers. This is a generated algorithm study; an
unrelated dataset would not clarify its assignment mechanism.

> What the checks establish

The original optimum is preserved, the conflict example actually changes an
earlier choice, and negative costs require the active-prefix argument rather
than globally feasible zero initialization. The two work families match their
exact operation formulas. General floating inputs still need scale-aware
residual assessment; forbidden edges, rectangular assignment and exact rational
certificates are outside this finite square solver.

> Historical sources

The historical sources are [H. W. Kuhn (1955), The Hungarian method for the
assignment problem](https://doi.org/10.1002/nav.3800020109), Naval Research
Logistics Quarterly 2, 83–97, and [James Munkres (1957), Algorithms for the
Assignment and Transportation Problems](https://doi.org/10.1137/0105003),
Journal of SIAM 5, 32–38. The implementation uses a compact ascending-column
augmenting-path potential variant, not a literal transcription of either paper's
presentation. The cubic bound here follows the displayed kernel's loops, not an
attribution of that particular implementation to the original publications.
